# UI test: `a[5..1]`, a slice whose literal bounds hold nothing (SC0154).
#
# The rule is literal-only on purpose. `a[n..m]` with `n` above `m` is the
# same empty slice and it is not reported here, because saying so needs the
# range analysis §1.3 hands to `SC0286` in the type checker. Drawing the line
# at what is written in the text is what keeps this free of false positives in
# the highest-traffic expression in the language.
#
# No `= help:`, and §7.1 says why in as many words: which of the two bounds is
# the wrong one is not something the compiler can know. The message says so,
# because a reader told only that the range is empty will re-read the bound
# they already believe.
#
# The inclusive form is the same mistake and is caught by the same rule.

def windows(samples: Array of Int) -> Int:
    let empty be samples[5..1]
    let also be samples[9..=2]
    return 0
